# Talking-point bullets for the "+ More" drawer. One line per bullet.
BUL={
1:"""Welcome. The chat is open the whole time, silly questions first.
A co-pilot (an AI helper) is watching the chat for me.
This is a case study: how we AI-enabled a team, told as a bridge we crossed together.""",
2:"""House rule: when buzzwords pile up, a hand goes up. "Stop with the jargon. Just talk to me."
Everyone in this room gets that hand today.
I'll define each AI term the first time I say it, in one plain sentence.""",
101:"""Hope 1: you leave with one idea you can try today.
Hope 2: permission to ask the question you think is too basic.
Hope 3: a reason to share your small wins.""",
3:"""Three phases to remember: Play (try it), Build (make it), Design (shape it).
Before is the survey and permits: concerns and goals.
After is opening day: the result and what we learned.
Like a real bridge: get ready, cross in three spans, arrive.""",
102:"""Leaders were excited, and said this is the way.
The CEO's line sets the tone: arguing for manual screwdrivers won't be much fun over the next 20 years.
It created immense pressure for everyone to figure out an AI use case.
Their energy was real and useful. It just wasn't the whole story.""",
7:"""The market was (and still is) racing, and everyone talked about it in jargon.
FOMO was real: AI has the best marketing teams working on it.""",
103:"""Our customers were highly technical and security-minded.
Trust was fragile: easy to shatter, brutal to rebuild.
Early chatbot experiments raised support tickets. Vague answers frustrated people fast.""",
104:"""The team was concerned, and they weren't wrong.
The worries: the environment, made-up answers (hallucinations), brain rot, ethics, being replaced.
Concerns like these are information, not resistance.""",
6:"""The skeptics and the racers weren't wrong. They just weren't talking to each other.
Our customers were our team, and empathetic teams spot concerns fast. That's what you want when the market says ship AI now.
It only works when people feel safe saying it out loud: psychological safety.
My job was to close this gap so we could all succeed together.""",
9:"""Goal: bridge from AI skeptic to AI native, together, in a meaningful way.
Everyone crosses the same bridge, in plain language, with nobody gatekept out.
Worries get heard, not dismissed, and the human stays in charge.
AI is no longer really an opt-out. Waiting stopped being the safe choice.
The seven planks are the story of how we crossed.""",
105:"""Before we built anything, we made a safe space to say the worries out loud.
The worries were real: environment, made-up answers, brain rot, ethics, data, accountability, craft, being replaced.
They got heard, not dismissed. "Will I be replaced?" was the one I addressed right away.
Psychological safety comes first: people can speak up without being punished.
If you call concerns resistance, people go quiet, and you lose the information you need most.""",
106:"""Pitfall: treating concerns as resistance. People go quiet.
When they go quiet, you lose the information you need most.
Get concerns into the open, then understand and empathize. Move through them, not around them.""",
107:"""Cool thing: saying things out loud led to team standards for AI, plus solutions and principles we could use to guide our work.
We knew we couldn't solve everything. But putting our empathetic brains on AI values and operations made our projects sharper and better.
Safety first, then speed.""",
15:"""Takeaway: AI isn't a side quest. It's meaningfully part of our team objectives.
Company goals were vague. They didn't tell a team what to do on Monday.
We planned and aligned across roadmaps to find the through-lines.
Then we created AI OKRs (a goal plus the measures that show you got there) that connected up to the company goals.
Leadership could see it, so AI wasn't a side project.""",
17:"""Objective: create AI experiences that help customers find answers faster.
Key results: improve search accuracy, keep customer sentiment positive, check the box on AI.
Team goals: level up on how AI really works, expand research to include AI, evolve the team for a new future.""",
108:"""Cool thing: our work showed up in the company's goals.
Team goals connected up through the roadmap to company goals.
Leadership could see it, so AI wasn't a side project.""",
18:"""Play was tied to the goals, but the goal was broad to start.
We still had an OKR for the quarter, part of the company's efficiency initiative.
It was about improving your own workflow, not other people's.
Low risk: it's your own work, so there's little to lose.
It gave space to just try things out. Psychological safety, not a scheduled block of time.""",
19:"""A shared prompt library for common documents like PRDs and design specs (a set of tested instructions, like recipe cards).
Drafting internal blogs, copyediting, and using AI as a sounding board.
Testing which models worked best for what. Not everything needs the biggest model.
Candid lesson: early chatbot experiments increased support tickets.""",
20:"""Takeaway: enable, don't gatekeep. Non-experts doing expert-level work, with the human still in charge.
Share: biweekly reviews and internal blogs kept everyone in sync. No one worked in the shadows.
Teach what we did and why, so others reuse the thinking and not just the output.
Celebrate: quarterly demos gave everyone a place to be seen.
Publish: docs, guidance and external blogs, so sharing and celebrating reached customers too.""",
109:"""Keep an eye out for experts who keep the keys: more access, more jargon.
Everyone else waits outside. That's another glass ceiling.
Gatekeeping hoards the tool, the vocabulary, or the reasoning.
Fix: share all three. Enable, don't gatekeep.""",
110:"""Cool thing: when people started to play and find value for themselves, it naturally led them to find value and opportunity for each other. A napkin idea became a real tool.
"Yes, and" is borrowed from improv: accept the idea, then add to it instead of shooting it down.
Sharing what worked led us to standardize it. That is what moved us from Play into Build.""",
22:"""Three tools, built by us for us. None were off the shelf.
Feedback tool: months of research became minutes.
Content scorer: one area's average score rose by about a third.
Papercut report: dozens of improvements in record time.
Candid: all three started small and rough. Next, a quick look at each.""",
38:"""What it does: pulls feedback from community posts, support tickets, surveys and social channels into one daily pipeline.
AI sorts every item by type, product and sentiment. A dashboard shows filters, sentiment trends and on-demand summaries.
Result: months of research became minutes, and more people got closer to customers. Digging through scattered feedback became strategy conversations shaped by customer signal.
What it took (candid): we first found a research repository nobody used, because nobody had time.
So we teamed up with machine learning engineers, built a content warehouse, refined it with invested stakeholders, and launched with meetings, case studies and demos.
It sits behind company login. Ten steps from problem to launch, none of them skipped.""",
39:"""What it does: scores customer-facing words (UI copy, error messages, docs) against the glossary, voice and tone, and UX writing best practices.
It started as a spreadsheet: formulas for readability, passive voice, action-led structure and inclusive language, rolled into one weighted score.
Formulas can't judge tone, so we added an AI layer that checks context and voice. Does the error message sound empathetic?
Result: one product area's average score went from 7.42 to 10, about a 35% lift. Manual line edits became high-judgment feedback that shaped how we approached design critique.
Candid: we rewrote every page description in that area by hand to prove it first. Then rebuilt it as a real app, with moderated usability tests and a 25-person survey before rollout.
It checks what you wrote instead of writing it for you. The human stays in charge.""",
40:"""Left: how the papercut report works, in six steps. Gather all the feedback, find and count the themes, check whether the docs already answer it, add customer evidence with full quotes, classify each theme as a bug, UX friction or docs gap, then write it up in plain English.
Right: what it set off. By the end of this work we were getting about 30 new UX improvement tickets every month.
A monthly cross-team review of open-ended survey feedback turned themes into tickets and tracked them: closed, in progress, backlog, needs triage.
Cool thing: the papercut report led to a lot more getting done. It made small problems visible and easy to prioritize, so they actually got fixed.
Candid: it took many rounds to get right. Early versions invented quotes, so we required full, real quotes.
A person still adds the tickets and decides what to fix. The AI finds and explains; we decide.""",
12:"""Pitfall: designers started acting as engineers.
A team of "I" leads to burnout. Everyone doing everything alone doesn't scale.
What we did: worked together, learned more about development and model training, reached out to experts.
Don't try to do it all alone.""",
111:"""Fix for the build pitfall: the paper napkin. Go run and do fun stuff, and test your ideas in a prototype.
Then throw it away and work as a team. Engineering teams will find more stable builds in code and see flaws you can't.
We work together on stable solutions.
Understand your value in the room, and understand others' value in the room. Don't try to do it all alone.""",
112:"""Cool thing: we got more technical.
We also got more cross-functional: designers, engineers, strategy and content, building side by side.
Show the tools. This is where the "wow" lives.""",
27:"""Pitfall: when things move fast, the basics get forgotten and the customer gets left out.
It's easy to get caught up in the new tools and the hype instead of the user's needs and the business problem.
That creates issues if no one is charged with watching out for it.
As strategists, we are the voice of the customer. We always put needs over hype.""",
113:"""Cool thing: the experiences got much better than "drop in a chatbot."
AI often means dropping in a chat bot. We got further because we knew what could happen.
Building first taught us where AI is strong, where it needs help, and where it doesn't belong.""",
122:"""We replaced documentation, tooltips and help with AI tips shown right where the customer is working.
Each tip uses a pre-written, stress-tested prompt tied to exactly that spot. Nothing open-ended.
We focused on high-traffic, high-support areas first.
Impact: sentiment and task completion went up. It actually helped people.
Candid: it grew out of an existing inline-content pattern. The real work was monitoring it.""",
23:"""Feedback: digging through scattered feedback became strategy conversations shaped by customer signal.
Scorer: manual line edits became high-judgment feedback that shaped how we approached design critique.
Papercuts: hunting small problems by hand became a systematic way to find and prioritize. It led to about 30 new UX improvement tickets a month, and a lot more getting done.
Mundane work to strategic work. That's the point of AI for us.""",
24:"""Having data in hand is a powerful tool for conversation, and AI lets you grab it.
Not being constantly in the weeds frees time for the bigger picture.
Teams like ours fight for a seat at the table. With AI the fight got tougher: every team felt they could jump over us with the speed and false confidence of AI.
Proudest moment: one of my direct reports was invited into annual planning conversations.""",
43:"""Section header: what we learned along the way.
The slides that follow list the learnings, one each.""",
28:"""Takeaway: share the tool, the words and the reasoning.
Build tools so non-experts can do expert-level work, with the human still in charge.
People review the output and ask an expert when unsure.
Add skills, don't replace specialists.""",
31:"""Takeaway: don't talk down to each other, and feel confident enough to ask others not to.
Almost no one is an AI expert, except engineers building models at their core.
When someone makes a big claim, ask what it does, who checked it, and how many moving parts it needs.
If you don't know, ask me or ask Copilot. It's fun to figure out.""",
114:"""Takeaway: if you keep prompting the same thing, ask AI if code or a script could do it.
Look for pragmatic engineering first. Not everything needs an LLM or an agent.
Ask AI how to build it, then try.""",
115:"""Takeaway: start a folder and save things: ideas, tools, patterns.
Designing with AI is a lot about curation.
I saved an open-source slide repo to my library for this very talk.""",
116:"""Takeaway: pay attention to the bigger picture.
When working with a new technology, ask questions constantly about the problem being solved.
It has to come back to impact or a measurement.
If we can't measure it, we shouldn't do it.""",
120:"""Takeaway: the first pancake sucks.
Your first prompt or tool won't be great. You'll tweak, edit and nudge. It takes time and can be frustrating.
Once one works, you've solved a future-you problem.
And your next tool starts smarter, because you know what went wrong the first time.""",
117:"""Takeaway: think about how AI could make the world better for the person you're solving for.
Start with one person, not a platform.
The power of teams like ours is deep empathy.
Solutions can come fast and be executed even faster. When you pinpoint a single person, keep your humanity intact.""",
119:"""Takeaway: share and celebrate openly. Everything is new and fresh.
Share: biweekly reviews and internal blogs kept everyone in sync. No one worked in the shadows.
Teach what we did and why, so others reuse the thinking and not just the output.
Celebrate: quarterly demos gave everyone a place to be seen.
Publish: docs, guidance and external blogs, so sharing and celebrating reached customers too.
The small things you're already doing are clever solutions.
Learn more about the tools you already have access to.""",
37:"""AI took down the language barrier.
The mundane work is what AI is for. The people in this room lead with judgment, empathy and context.
We crossed the bridge together.""",
36:"""Drop your questions in the chat. Silly ones first.
The best plain-English ones get answered live.
If jargon slips out, call it and I'll restate it.""",

123:"""This is what the papercut report actually does, in six steps.
Gather all the feedback in one place: support tickets, community posts, surveys, docs questions.
Find the themes and count how often each one comes up. Each person only counts once.
Check whether the docs already answer it, and add community threads and full customer quotes as evidence.
Classify each theme: a bug, UX friction, or a docs gap. That decides who gets it.
Write it up in plain English so a stakeholder can act on it. Bugs go to engineering, UX issues to design, docs gaps to content.
Candid: it took many rounds to get right. Early versions invented quotes, so we required full, real quotes."""
,
124:"""This is the first report we ran, on one stuck point in onboarding.
It turned about nine hundred pieces of feedback into twelve themes and thirteen tickets.
Three bugs raised, five fixes already closed, and five waiting in the backlog.
The point: feedback stopped being a pile of complaints and became a to-do list the team could ship.
Cool thing: a person still adds the tickets and decides what to fix. The AI finds and explains, we decide."""
,
}

BUL[130]="Your turn: a two-minute AI tip.\nYou already use Copilot to chat. Today it builds you a tiny app.\nPaste the prompt, copy the code into TextEdit, make it plain text (Shift-Cmd-T), save as tool.html with the “use .txt” box unchecked, double-click it.\nFirst try a bit rough? That's the first pancake. Ask it to fix it."
BUL[131]='Where this goes next, at One North: the rules for products we build and advise on.\nPurpose over novelty: start with the problem, not the shiny tool.\nBuilding over prompting: ask if code can do it.\nAppropriateness over adoption: for customer experiences, AI is optional when possible.\nConservation over consumption: use the smallest tool that does the job.\nAccountability over automation: a person owns every result.'

# --- Nov 11 trim: speaker notes from the outline doc ---
BUL[22]="Sharing what worked moved us from Play into Build. But build with a reason, not because you can.\nThe test, three questions. Who asked for this? Does it bring in something we couldn't see before? Is it one thing built well, or many things built fast?\nAnti-pattern: a chatbot for every problem. We didn't want that.\nTwo tools, built by us for us. None were off the shelf.\nFeedback tool: months of research became minutes.\nContent scorer: one area's average score rose by about a third.\nCandid: both started small and rough. Next, a quick look at each."
BUL[38]="What it does: pulls feedback from community posts, support tickets, surveys and social channels into one daily pipeline.\nAI sorts every item by type, product and sentiment. A dashboard shows filters, sentiment trends and on-demand summaries.\nResult: months of research became minutes, and more people got closer to customers. Digging through scattered feedback became strategy conversations shaped by customer signal.\nWhat it took (candid): we first found a research repository nobody used, because nobody had time.\nSo we teamed up with machine learning engineers, built a content warehouse, refined it with invested stakeholders, and launched with meetings, case studies and demos.\nIt sits behind company login. Ten steps from problem to launch, none of them skipped.\nThe test: who asked for this? [your answer]. Something we couldn't see before: customer signal from every channel in one place. One thing built well, not many things built fast."
BUL[39]="What it does: scores customer-facing words (UI copy, error messages, docs) against the glossary, voice and tone, and UX writing best practices.\nIt started as a spreadsheet: formulas for readability, passive voice, action-led structure and inclusive language, rolled into one weighted score.\nFormulas can't judge tone, so we added an AI layer that checks context and voice. Does the error message sound empathetic?\nResult: one product area's average score went from 7.42 to 10, about a 35% lift. Manual line edits became high-judgment feedback that shaped how we approached design critique.\nCandid: we rewrote every page description in that area by hand to prove it first. Then rebuilt it as a real app, with moderated usability tests and a 25-person survey before rollout.\nIt checks what you wrote instead of writing it for you. The human stays in charge.\nThe test: who asked for this? [your answer]. Something we couldn't see before: how every page really scored on voice and tone. One thing built well: proven by hand first, then rebuilt as a real app with tests."
BUL[12]='Pitfall: designers started acting as engineers.\nA team of "I" leads to burnout. Everyone doing everything alone doesn\'t scale.\nWhat we did: worked together, learned more about development and model training, reached out to experts.\nDon\'t try to do it all alone.'
BUL[111]="Fix for the build pitfall: know your lane, and embrace your specific skills as a team.\nBuild as a team. Everyone has to do their job and own their role.\nUX can spot the missing user value. The engineer can spot the build issues. The product manager can see that the idea won't sell.\nWe work together on stable solutions. Skills are magnified, not replaced.\nHuman in the loop (a person checks and decides) is about taste, creativity and authority.\nDon't worry about claiming tasks. The human superpower is talking to experts and making the judgment call.\nBring in the experts and build it the right way.\nUnderstand your value in the room, and understand others' value in the room. Don't try to do it all alone."
BUL[112]='Cool thing: we got more technical.\nWe also got more cross-functional: designers, engineers, strategy and content, building side by side.\nPurpose-built tools are great. We build in GitHub.\nMake internal systems reachable by LLMs instead of spawning a chatbot per problem.\nShow the tools. This is where the "wow" lives.'
BUL[25]="Both tools showed us something we couldn't see before. Design is what we did with that sight.\nDesign: learn by doing. You don't know what AI can and can't do until you work with it.\nPeople who only have the theory often never start. You can't learn to swim from a book.\nBecause we built first, we knew where AI was strong and where it needed help.\nTwo ways we designed AI differently: 1. Papercuts, where the signals from our tools come together. 2. Watch the answers, fix the source: prompt monitoring and chunkability, designed behind the scenes."
BUL[40]="Where it fits: the two tools we just built feed it, the feedback tool and the content scorer, plus survey responses. It's connected straight to the dashboard.\nTwo kinds of signal, one place. This is where they come together.\nLeft: how the papercut report works, in six steps. Gather all the feedback, find and count the themes, check whether the docs already answer it, add community evidence with full quotes, classify each theme as a bug, UX friction or docs gap, then write it up in plain English.\nRight: what it set off. By the end of this work we were getting about 30 new UX improvement tickets every month.\nA monthly cross-team review of open-ended survey feedback turned themes into tickets and tracked them: closed, in progress, backlog, needs triage.\nCool thing: the papercut report led to a lot more getting done. It made small problems visible and easy to prioritize, so they actually got fixed.\nCandid: it took many rounds to get right. Early versions invented quotes, so we required full, real quotes.\nA person still adds the tickets and decides what to fix. The AI finds and explains; we decide."
BUL[126]="Papercuts made small problems visible. Now point the same eyes at the AI itself.\nWay 2, designing behind the scenes: prompt monitoring and chunkability. One loop: watch the answers, fix the source. This is the work I most want you to see.\nWatch: we built monitoring so we knew what was happening. We tracked prompts, sentiment and accuracy over time. Because we had built things first, we knew enough to monitor the responses, not just ship and hope. It passes the test: it shows us what the AI is really saying, which we couldn't see before.\nWhat it showed: the AI answers were a 50/50 split of thumbs up and thumbs down. People told us third-party AI tools gave them a mixed bag too.\nThe data pointed at the content, not the prompt. A hallucination is the AI confidently making something up. The AI was pulling from the wrong material, and the more information it had, the less accurate it got.\nOur docs were written for people who use sidebars and context. AI reads them in small slices, called chunks. We can't control how another tool slices our docs, so we assume the worst: no title, no breadcrumbs, no neighbors.\nThe stranger test: if I handed this slice to a stranger, would they know exactly which product and which action it's about?\nFix: we built a skill that scores a page: Robust is 22 to 26, Stable is 16 to 21, Fragile is under 16. First audit, three key decision pages: scores of 19, 15 and 16. None were Robust, and one failed a hard floor.\nPlan: baseline test, fix the pages and re-test the same questions, then target the pages with the most thumbs down.\nResult: answers went from about one in five accurate to about four in five. Content is king.\nThis was an actual agent experience. Chat was one more way to interface with it and take action on the real products. It was not an LLM wrapper having basic conversations. Without a strategy behind the content, it falls back to a bad chatbot."
BUL[27]="Content is king. Hype over the customer is how you forget it.\nPitfall: when things move fast, the basics get forgotten and the customer gets left out.\nIt's easy to get caught up in the new tools and the hype instead of the user's needs and the business problem.\nThat creates issues if no one is charged with watching out for it.\nAs strategists, we are the voice of the customer. We always put needs over hype."
BUL[132]="That's the basic way this works. A first pancake. Now think of how you could make this better.\nImagine the design system is ready and connected to an outline for a talk. It can even look at the speaker's previous talks to refine its answers. That gets fun fast.\nSpec it. Plan mode (an AI mode that plans before it builds) can interview you until we have a clear plan, or product requirements doc.\nWork agile: small loops, and use your judgment.\nAdd a success metric. The goal: remove as much of the ability to hallucinate as possible.\nThere's a black box of AI. As we build and use it, we shrink it as far as we can. You get better the more you do it.\nThis is the magic of AI: it breaks down the language barrier, and then you start layering tools.\nNow imagine you have this cool bingo card that you want to create custom for every meeting, based on the host. That's when this all becomes a skill: the recurring instructions.\nSkill versus agent is a common mix-up. The agent is Claude or Copilot, the worker. The skill is the recipe it follows.\nA true agent would run without being asked: watch the calendar, spot the meeting, look up the host, build the card and attach it to the invite.\nThe test: if you start it and it follows saved steps, it's a skill. If it starts itself and picks its own steps, it's an agent.\nNow, how I got my team to this point."

BUL[31]="""Takeaway: everyone is still figuring this out, so don't talk down to each other, and feel confident enough to ask others not to.
True AI experts are very few: the engineers building the models and the data scientists studying them.
Everyone else is on a line of how much time they've spent playing. Developers have a head start on the language, not a monopoly on it.
It's not a select group. It's a bit of time and a few key concepts.
Stay curious: ask each other how it works.
If you don't know, ask me or ask Copilot. It's fun to figure out."""
BUL[109]="""Keep an eye out for experts who keep the keys: more access, more jargon.
Everyone else waits outside. That's another glass ceiling.
Gatekeeping is toxic. These are the people who show the results but not the will to share.
Gatekeeping hoards the tool, the vocabulary, or the reasoning.
Fix: share all three. Enable, don't gatekeep."""

BUL[109]="""Pitfall: don't forget to come up for air. AI gets addictive.
People can spend hours on something they could ask a developer about and get done in two seconds. Ask early.
The flip side: if you're the one with the answer, share it fast.
Keep an eye out for experts who keep the keys: more access, more jargon. Everyone else waits outside. That's another glass ceiling.
Gatekeeping is toxic. These are the people who show the results but not the will to share.
Fix: share all three, the tool, the vocabulary and the reasoning. Enable, don't gatekeep."""

BUL[132]="""That's the basic way this works. A first pancake. Now think of how you could make this better.
Imagine the design system is ready and connected to an outline for a talk. It can even look at the speaker's previous talks to refine its answers. That gets fun fast.
Spec it. Plan mode (an AI mode that plans before it builds) can interview you until we have a clear plan, or product requirements doc.
Work agile: small loops, and use your judgment.
Add a success metric. The goal: remove as much of the ability to hallucinate as possible.
There's a black box of AI. As we build and use it, we shrink it as far as we can. You get better the more you do it.
This is the magic of AI: it breaks down the language barrier, and then you start layering tools.
Now imagine you have this cool bingo card that you want to create custom for every meeting, based on the host. Here's how it evolves.
1. A prompt: paste it, get one card.
2. Code: keep re-pasting the same prompt? Ask AI if a script could do it. The shuffle doesn't need an LLM at all. Not everything does.
3. A skill: the recurring instructions for a card per host. You start it, and it follows saved steps.
4. A workflow: fixed steps chained together. Get the host, look up their past talks, build the card, save the file. Same path every time.
5. An agent: you say "make the card for tomorrow's meeting" and it picks its own steps. It checks the calendar, finds the host, decides which talks to read, and redoes the card if it looks off. A model with tools, running in a loop.
Skill versus agent is a common mix-up. The agent is Claude or Copilot, the worker. The skill is the recipe it follows.
The test: who picks the steps? If you do, it's a skill or a workflow. If it does, it's an agent.
Don't build an agent for everything. They cost more, and mistakes matter more. A bingo card is low stakes and the path is known, so a skill or workflow is the right tool. Agents are for ambiguous, valuable work you can't map in advance.
Now, how I got my team to this point."""
